# Mission Control — Agentic OS (v1)

An operating-system-style command center for supervising, controlling, connecting, inspecting, routing, and preserving multiple AI agent systems from one interface. Codename **Mission Control**. Owner context: VRN / PortMontage.

This is a shell. Every AI system is a registered **connector** with a live, openable, controllable control room. Nothing here talks to a real service yet — every connector is a typed mock adapter that simulates latency, streaming output, and state changes — but the architecture is built so real adapters attach later without a UI rewrite.

Connector work must follow [AGENTIC_OS_BUILD_POLICY.md](./AGENTIC_OS_BUILD_POLICY.md).

## Running it

```bash
npm install
npm run dev
```

Open http://localhost:3000. `npm run build` produces a clean production build (no TypeScript errors).

## Mental model

- The **sidebar is the System Registry**, not navigation chrome. It renders directly from `ConnectorRegistry` — every entry is a real connector, openable at `/systems/[id]`.
- **UI never talks to a tool directly.** Every screen calls methods on the `AgentSystemAdapter` interface (`src/lib/connectors/types.ts`). Today that interface is backed by a mock transport; tomorrow it's a real CLI/API/MCP bridge. No component contains agent-specific logic.
- **Obsidian (the Shared Brain) is a first-class system**, not a note widget. Every other connector has a `memoryWriteObligation` and routes its report/approval writes through the Obsidian adapter.
- **`AGENT_SESSION_INDEX.md`** is a single, append-only, human-readable global session registry. Agents can only append to it — an overwrite attempt is converted into an `ApprovalRequest` instead of executing (see `vaultEngine.createSessionRegistryEntry` in `src/lib/connectors/mock/obsidian-instance.ts`). If a system has no direct write path, the Shared Brain page can generate a copy/paste-ready block for the operator instead.

