## Active vs. future connectors

The System Registry holds two kinds of entries, merged by `src/lib/connectors/directory.ts`:

- **8 active connectors** — real `AgentSystemAdapter` implementations in `src/lib/connectors/mock/`. Openable at `/systems/[id]`, with a live control room, streaming output, approvals, and reports.
- **26 future/pending connectors** — plain `FutureConnectorDescriptor` records in `src/mocks/future-connectors.ts`. No adapter, no mock transport, no control room. They exist to prove the registry scales, not to simulate a live agent. Clicking one (in the Sidebar or `/registry`) opens a details panel showing its category, planned connection modes, and planned capabilities — it never opens `/systems/[id]` or any control-room UI.
- **34 connectors visible in total**, grouped into the 13 required categories (AI Agents, Orchestrators, Automation, Browser Agents, Knowledge / Memory, Project Management, Communication, Code / Dev Tools, Databases, File Systems, APIs / Webhooks, Governance / Proof, Monitoring / Logs) — 2 future connectors per category, plus the 8 actives layered on top of 5 of those categories.

Both the Sidebar and `/registry` render from `getConnectorDirectory()` / `groupByCategory()` in `directory.ts` — neither component branches on a specific connector id, active or future. Both include a search box (`name`, `category`, `capability` substring match) and category grouping/filtering.

Promoting a future connector to active requires:
1. One adapter file implementing `AgentSystemAdapter` (any file in `mock/` is a template)
2. One line added to `src/lib/connectors/registry.ts`
3. Optionally, one seed mock data file
4. Then delete its entry from `future-connectors.ts`

Zero UI redesign — the Sidebar, `/registry`, Operator Home, Approvals, Evidence, and Work Queue all already render from the same adapter/summary interfaces.
